#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124271 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data"}] :question/id #uuid "e8f30d47-bfd2-50ea-b2ad-d474e2a58d29" :question/math-academy-id "e-5598" :question/problem "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124272 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction"}] :question/id #uuid "95a45716-8c29-5955-af3c-43b6344912da" :question/math-academy-id "e-5599" :question/problem "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A$.\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A$, while $400$ customers use sugar $B$. How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}600 \\\\ 400\\end{bmatrix}$\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000)$, we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}600 \\\\ 400\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= Px_{0} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A$. Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000$, we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124281 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a State Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "09540ff7-0444-5169-b582-471be2f1972d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5971" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2$." :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2$, we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= Px_{0} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= Px_{1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124343 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "25622a0e-3e5b-5f09-bef6-afaa30d153d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9162" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1$. A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix}0.35 + 0.65 = 1; 0.95 + 0.05 = 1$\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\bf{V}$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa30e03b-73c6-58a8-8ca2-d33652dd55f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121136} {:answer/id #uuid "345be07d-f682-5e56-9287-b3b6e80a5244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121137} {:answer/id #uuid "40c12b32-1184-5eef-88e6-3794bd5165c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$, $v$, and $w$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121138} {:answer/id #uuid "76af5fa2-a466-5fc8-bf49-da98e388d9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121139} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6750c6-50ee-5544-af49-77996fcd6cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e6750c6-50ee-5544-af49-77996fcd6cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121140} :answer-field/id #uuid "c728a98d-78c3-51e5-8097-83a2c3ff278a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121135}] :question/id #uuid "6d6371d4-1a60-5526-b7b1-69a38ead3549" :question/math-academy-id "q-109881" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$u = \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}-0.45 \\\\ 1.55\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76f2582-fea2-5626-98ba-82d34f627a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122063} {:answer/id #uuid "cfba4be1-9c4a-5c04-be11-f069e3cf46f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122064} {:answer/id #uuid "dd42c5e0-7138-583a-a92f-19729c14db8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "640000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122065} {:answer/id #uuid "eeec030c-7b86-58d4-b092-fe58988f8a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "430000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122066} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc2e0e9-2a0a-5c8c-8ab7-51db96111980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "520000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b76f2582-fea2-5626-98ba-82d34f627a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122063} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb82541c-1e5d-58a7-bb27-5fed853fe87b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122062}] :question/id #uuid "62389cec-4c75-591c-8976-ec4ed362e628" :question/math-academy-id "q-35845" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B$. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30%$ of Phone $B$ customers 